WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:06.120
DC's Titans has earned a mostly positive reaction
from fans and critics, but DC Universe's flagship

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.050
series is far from perfect.

00:00:08.050 --> 00:00:11.920
These are our picks for the five best - and
five worst - things about Titans so far.

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:13.010
Best: The Nuclear Family

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:17.439
Titans season 1 introduced villains the Nuclear
Family, who quickly established themselves

00:00:17.439 --> 00:00:20.539
as being funny, creepy, and absolutely vicious.

00:00:20.539 --> 00:00:24.750
The Nuclear Family of the comics were somewhat
different; they had a different origin, different

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:26.929
powers, and never actually locked horns with
the Titans.

00:00:26.929 --> 00:00:32.329
Their first appearance was 1985's The Outsiders
#1, where they were actually all robots.

00:00:32.329 --> 00:00:36.640
Despite their safe, suburban appearance, these
villains are truly brutal on the show, going

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:39.000
so far as putting Dove in a lengthy coma.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.070
Thematically, they're perfect adversaries
for the Titans.

00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:46.550
Just about all the Titans are either orphans,
runaways, or both, while the sadistic Nuclears

00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:50.470
are a twisted parody of what society would
have us believe is a "true" family.

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:52.410
"Hold your f---ing' horses."

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:53.410
"Honey!

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:54.400
Language."

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:56.180
"Sorry."

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:57.160
Worst: Beast Boy's powers

00:00:57.170 --> 00:01:00.900
Ryan Potter does a good job as Gar Logan,
a.k.a. Beast Boy.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:04.180
But fans of the character have still been
frustrated by how limited his powers have

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:05.210
seemed so far.

00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:08.400
In the comics, Gar can turn into just about
any kind of animal.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:11.750
It doesn't matter if the animal is native
to Earth or even if it's ever existed.

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:16.300
Yet for all of Titans' first season, Gar only
uses his powers to change into a tiger.

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:19.670
"Tigers have always been my favorite, ever
since I was a kid."

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:23.670
The show has a justification for Gar's limited
skill set, but it still takes away from the

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:26.670
fun of wondering what Beast Boy will change
into next.

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:28.860
The situation is starting to get better, though.

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:33.030
In the season 2 premiere, shortly after the
Trigon-possessed Titans beat Gar to a pulp,

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:34.600
we see him change into a snake.

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:37.300
Hopefully we'll see some more variety in the
episodes to come.

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:38.300
Best: Jason Todd

00:01:38.300 --> 00:01:42.690
Curran Walters first appeared as Jason Todd
- AKA the new Robin - halfway through Titans'

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:46.560
first season, and it wasn't clear if the young
braggart would be a regular feature.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:50.190
But Jason shows up in the season 2 premiere,
and by the end of the episode he's joined

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:52.560
the team at Titans Tower in San Francisco.

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:53.670
"You're the new Robin?"

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:55.580
"Present and accounted for, babe."

00:01:57.729 --> 00:02:02.180
Jason adds an unstable element to the Titans
- he's hot-blooded, doesn't respect authority,

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:03.869
and doesn't play well with others.

00:02:03.869 --> 00:02:06.760
And comics readers know that he's a ticking
time bomb.

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:11.009
Jason was famously killed by the Joker in
the classic "Death in the Family" storyline,

00:02:11.009 --> 00:02:13.780
returning years later as the lethal vigilante
Red Hood.

00:02:13.780 --> 00:02:15.010
Worst: Where's the action?

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:17.280
Titans is pretty light on action.

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:20.580
And when a fight scene does kick off, it's
often a little underwhelming.

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:24.480
Titans is good at more grounded action, like
scenes of Dick Grayson taking on a bunch of

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:25.870
thugs in an alleyway.

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:30.370
But the team now includes a girl with demonic
powers, a boy who can change into deadly beasts,

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:34.970
a fighter comparable to Wonder Woman, and
a super-strong alien who can hurl energy blasts

00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:35.970
at her foes.

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:39.010
So why are so many fights just simple hand-to-hand
combat?

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:42.319
It's rare to even see more than two or three
of the heroes fighting together.

00:02:42.319 --> 00:02:47.260
Granted, Titans is still just a TV show, and
you can't expect big-budget special effects.

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:50.740
But what's the point of a superhero team-up
series if we don't get to see them all teaming

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:51.900
up for a giant fight?

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:53.060
Best: Hawk &amp; Dove

00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:57.030
Despite their silly costumes, the story of
Hawk and Dove has proven to be one of Titans'

00:02:57.030 --> 00:02:58.310
most compelling arcs.

00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:01.860
Some of the best episodes of the first season
focused on their different motives for becoming

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:06.280
crimefighters, Hawk's struggle with drug addiction,
and Dove's brief romance with Dick Grayson.

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:08.830
Hopefully we'll see more of the pair in the
second season and beyond.

00:03:08.830 --> 00:03:09.830
Worst: An empty universe

00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:14.330
DC's most powerful superheroes exist in the
universe of Titans, but it doesn't always

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:15.420
feel like it.

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:19.310
All the big names are sidelined, and the longer
the show goes on, the more conspicuous their

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:20.310
absences feel.

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:22.790
"Big house, it's funny that I only ever see
two of you.

00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:25.860
It's almost like the studio couldn't afford
another X-Man."

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:29.650
We're already seeing the show's creative team
struggling to explain the absence of characters

00:03:29.650 --> 00:03:33.200
from the expanded universe, and not just the
ones in capes.

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:36.900
In the season 2 premiere when Hawk &amp; Dove
go to the Wayne Mansion looking for Batman,

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:39.340
Jason Todd has to break some bad news.

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:42.739
"Bruce is halfway across with the world with
the Justice League.

00:03:42.739 --> 00:03:43.959
Alfred, too."

00:03:43.959 --> 00:03:45.519
Wait - Alfred, too?

00:03:45.519 --> 00:03:48.470
Why would Alfred need to go on assignment
with the Justice League?

00:03:48.470 --> 00:03:49.520
They can't make their own tea?

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:50.730
Best: The season 1 finale

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:54.400
It may have had a frustrating cliffhanger,
but the season 1 finale was by far one of

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:56.120
Titans' best episodes.

00:03:56.120 --> 00:04:00.069
Trigon subjects Dick to a nightmare vision
of his old mentor finally snapping and going

00:04:00.069 --> 00:04:04.330
on a rampage throughout Gotham City, with
Dick being the only one who can stop him - which

00:04:04.330 --> 00:04:05.450
he does.

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:06.450
Permanently.

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:10.730
The decision to give in to his most violent
urges puts Dick under Trigon's control.

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:14.690
Dick's adventure in the illusory Gotham is
an example of how Titans uses the expanded

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:16.160
universe to its advantage.

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:20.470
We only see glimpses of characters like Batman,
Joker, and Two-Face, but in this episode at

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:21.880
least, it's all we need to see.

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:22.880
Worst: That Bruce Wayne casting

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:27.050
The only A-list superhero we've actually seen
on Titans is Bruce Wayne, who shows up for

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:29.150
the first time in the season 2 premiere.

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:33.080
You'd be hard-pressed to challenge Iain Glen's
talent, especially after his memorable turn

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:35.260
as Jorah Mormont on Game of Thrones.

00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:38.820
but it's questionable whether or not he's
right for this particular role.

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:43.420
For one, his American accent isn't the most
convincing thing in the world, and at 58 years

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:47.880
old, he also seems just a little bit too old
to be playing the Dark Knight.

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:50.250
Glen isn't too old to play a retired Batman.

00:04:50.250 --> 00:04:52.440
But one who's still active with the Justice
League?

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:53.440
That's a stretch.

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:54.440
Best: Teasing Nightwing

00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:58.970
In the comics, Dick Grayson eventually comes
out from Batman's shadow, sets his Robin costume

00:04:58.970 --> 00:05:01.910
aside, and rebrands himself as the hero Nightwing.

00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:06.400
As Nightwing, Dick has a darker, less Peter-Pan-esque
costume and he even eventually leaves Gotham

00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:09.630
to find his own stomping grounds in the fictional
city of Blüdhaven.

00:05:09.630 --> 00:05:13.440
Titans has been teasing this development since
the first season, and it seems like just a

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:17.470
matter of time before Dick adopts the new
duds.

00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:18.470
Worst: Trigon's defeat

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:21.810
Toward the end of 2018, Titans showrunner
Greg Walker confirmed that the season 2 premiere

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:25.080
episode, "Trigon," was originally meant to
be the season 1 finale.

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:28.600
He claimed this was changed because he loved
the idea of ending the first season with a

00:05:28.600 --> 00:05:31.139
cliffhanger and starting the next with a bang.

00:05:31.139 --> 00:05:34.650
But after watching the episode, you might
wonder if the actual reason was because the

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:37.229
culmination of the story is just isn't satisfying.

00:05:37.229 --> 00:05:42.060
In "Trigon," almost every hero besides Rachel
and Gar do nothing to contribute to the main

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:43.520
villain's defeat.

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:47.150
Most of the heroes get possessed and just
stand around, looking possessed.

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:51.520
Rachel insists she has to free Dick from possession
before fighting Trigon, but it's not like

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:52.611
she really needs his help.

00:05:52.611 --> 00:05:56.770
How does she defeat the all-powerful demon
we've been told can destroy the universe?

00:05:56.770 --> 00:06:01.400
She walks up to him, says something snarky,
unleashes her power, and just kind of vaguely…

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:02.400
wins.

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:05.100
How she beats him isn't really spelled out
at all.

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:06.880
So much for that storyline.

